  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic overview of how soft or nanoscale materials can be made responsive through reversible physical interactions and more permanent chemical changes. On the left, “physical” mechanisms show structures changing state without breaking covalent backbones: polymer networks swell or shrink, surface charge is converted, small components assemble or dissociate into larger objects, and materials switch between sol and gel states. It also illustrates molecular recognition processes such as competitive binding and host–guest interactions, along with membrane-like events including fusion and disruption.\n\nOn the right, “chemical” mechanisms involve actual changes in molecular connectivity or composition. These include degradation of a network into fragments, dissociation of a micelle-like aggregate, crosslinking of separate units into a cluster, and removal of capping groups from a particle surface. Together these examples suggest different routes for controlling size, shape, aggregation, and surface properties.\n\nThe bottom-right panel summarizes the basic molecular operations that enable these responses: linking or cleaving bonds, binding or unbinding, changing charge state, altering hydrophobicity, and pressure-driven size changes. Overall, the image conveys a toolbox for designing stimuli-responsive materials by combining simple molecular transformations with larger structural transitions.",
